/*
here we go we are now making multiple files and modules
so this is actually really simple
there are 2 ways things can be handled in dyn

firstly we can export things that we want to make available to other files

secondly we could also encapslate them in different modules/namespaces instead too

we could actually have namespaces too?

I like the idea of doing import { x , y, z } from './filename'

is that how we can manage different libs?

like in order to make a lib you can create a series of fns that get wrapped in a module?

nah I think its possible for them to create them and just import from the folder that was linked in the dependencies

also the way I have workspaces and dependencies working are just letting the folders get included and we can make the paths easier to import by including them in the build as packages

so there are actually 2 things going on

separate pieces in different files

lib code

providing different examples here
*/

// you can define a name to container them like in react or others

// heres how to import

// can import individual pieces
import { addTwo, addOne } from './math.dyn'

// this brings both fns into scope so you can call addTwo or addOne

// or you can import like this
import './math.dyn' as Math

// this means you can do Math::addTwo()

// This means we can differentiate libs from separate files

// but here we go something to think about

// do we want to do that or do we want import * as Math from './math.dyn'

// first is less verbose and does the same thing, and if you want to specify then specify

import { addTwo }, * as Math from './math.dyn' // this will bring addTwo into scope and Math::addOne

// now on to libs and separate folders we can define

// import from a single file
import { addOne, addTwo, * } from './math.dyn'; // * in { } will bring all fns into scope 

// import from lib
import { * } from 'lib1'; // specifying lib1 here gets checked during build

// if there are conflicting item names in scope (2 of the same fn or type) then the compiler will error and youll need to alias them with names
import { xFn as x2, others } from 'somePlace'; // like so

void main() {
	// to call what we imported we can just do fn name from lib, we can do addOne, addTwo, or if we aliased we can do Alias::fn(); Only instances can do . accessing
}
